# Experiment Design

## 1. Research Question

Can successful reasoning and tool-use trajectories discovered through
test-time search be distilled into a model's more stable single-pass
reasoning behavior through supervised fine-tuning and verifiable
reinforcement learning?

---

## 2. Experimental Pipeline

starting policy
→ best-of-N test-time search
→ verified trajectories
→ trajectory SFT
→ GRPO / RLVR
→ controlled evaluation and ablation

---

## 3. Model Roles

### Qwen3.5-0.8B — Development Model

The 0.8B model is used for rapid iteration and end-to-end pipeline
validation.

It is responsible for validating:

- dataset preprocessing
- chat template construction
- trajectory serialization
- baseline evaluation
- SFT training
- reward computation
- group sampling
- GRPO training
- checkpointing
- logging and evaluation

The goal of this stage is not to maximize benchmark performance.

Instead, it answers:

> Does the entire post-training pipeline work correctly?

---

### Qwen3.5-4B — Formal Experiment Model

The 4B model is used for formal experiments after the pipeline has been
validated on the smaller model.

Primary experiments include:

- formal baseline evaluation
- best-of-N search
- trajectory SFT
- post-SFT evaluation
- ablation studies
- error analysis

This stage answers:

> Does the training recipe produce meaningful improvements on a more
> capable model?

---

## 4. Why Two Model Scales?

The training algorithms are fundamentally the same across the 0.8B and
4B models.

The smaller model is not used because it requires a different training
method. It is used because it enables faster iteration and cheaper
failure during pipeline development.

The 4B model is introduced only after the infrastructure and training
logic have been validated.

This avoids spending most of the compute budget debugging large-model
training runs.

---

## 5. GRPO Scope

GRPO is first validated on Qwen3.5-0.8B.

The smoke test verifies:

- multi-response sampling
- reward computation
- group-relative advantage estimation
- policy loss computation
- gradient updates
- checkpointing and evaluation

Formal 4B GRPO is not required for the initial project milestone.

The RTX 4060 environment is intentionally not used to force a large-scale
4B GRPO run under unrealistic memory constraints.

If additional compute becomes available, 4B GRPO can be added as an
extension experiment.

---

## 6. Compute Policy

RTX 4060:

Qwen3.5-0.8B
- SFT: allowed
- GRPO smoke test: allowed
- evaluation: allowed

Qwen3.5-4B
- quantized inference: allowed
- best-of-N search: allowed
- SFT: allowed when feasible with LoRA / QLoRA
- formal GRPO: disabled locally

---

## 7. Experimental Principle

Development experiments optimize for:

- correctness
- reproducibility
- iteration speed

Formal experiments optimize for:

- controlled comparison
- evaluation quality
- ablation
- interpretability of results

The development model should not be excessively hyperparameter-tuned.